Ray Dillinger <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
> zahlman@gmail.com wrote:
>
>> I'm not sure what the intent of posting is, then. Is this a call for
>> more developers? Just a string of YANI? Something else?
>
> Something else.  This is a request for reactions, to see whether 
> there is/would be community support for a successor to nethack 
> "the way I'd make it."  

That part of your thread opener was well hidden. ;-)

> I have a roguelike engine mostly written already.  It's advanced 
> enough in design to easily support a nethack successor (or something 
> even more complex) and modular enough that you could make nearly 
> arbitrary patches that *NEVER* interfere with each other.  I am 
> inordinately pleased with it.
>
> I'll be making a game on top of it, of course. 
>
> My question here was more about whether it's worth my time and effort
> to make that game explicitly a nethack successor (copy most of the 
> monster and item lists, player races and classes, claustrophobic 
> 23x79 dungeon levels, copy features like Vlad's tower and Fort Ludios 
> and Rodney, make it a Fetch-The-Amulet-of-Yendor Quest with a vibrating 
> square and elemental planes, etc......).  

In my opinion you could cut down on the classes.

There are about five really different classes right now in NetHack:
combat wombats,
spellcasters,
dagger throwers,
bare handed fighters (Monks) and
can-almost-do-nothing-good (Tourists)

> I could do that - modulo a few changes, as outlined, that I'd make 
> because, well, it's mine and I consider copying those things which 
> I consider "bad behavior" or "bad design" into my game to be 
> dealbreakers, things that I would never do. 

I'm not sure if this is the right place to ask. I don't think that
this group features a representative player set.

NetHack gets downloaded 13'000 times a month from sourceforge alone,
this group has 1588 members and 101 recent authors (determined by
google groups).

But besides the IRC channel I don't know a place with a NetHack
community big enough.

> Alternatively, I could make a completely different game and let the 
> nethack community continue to wait for the next version from the 
> devteam built on the devteam's extant codebase.  

I guess that there are players who would oppose a NetHack successor
that would not be a fork from the current code base.

But I wouldn't mind if the game is good and offers more or a different
game play than the current NetHack.

> Nah.  Gnomes, dwarves, elves, et al are out of legend far older than 
> Tolkien.  They'd stay.  Hobbits, yes, would be out.

You could name them Halflings or Lochwohner (according to Wikipedia
hole-dwellers = Holbytlan in Rohirrim -> Hobbit) or something like
that. :)

> Orc as a humanoid
> monster seems to originate with Tolkien, but there's no copyright 
> because Orc as a monster originates with (I believe) Pliny or 
> earlier sources.  I might de-Tolkienize them somewhat, but unless you 
> read flavor text you'd probably never notice the difference.  

I wonder in what way you could "de-Tolkienize" NetHacks orcs besides
renaming/removing the Mordor orc and the Uruk-hai.

NetHacks orcs are much more like the generic orcs from modern fantasy
than Tolkiens orcs.

>> 1) Conduct changed to be selected a priori, and enforced. 
>
> You want the game to enforce that you never break a selected conduct.  
> Not even accidentally.  I don't think this is a good plan.  Your 
> conduct characters are delving in the same dungeon as everyone else; 
> they have the same opportunities.  Their "conduct" is in what they 
> choose, each and every time that opportunity is there.  

And the needed code for enforcing the conducts is rather a waste of
time IMO. If you want to do a conduct, it should be hard.

But it would be nice to be able to pre-select a conduct to get the
necessary items in your start inventory (for example a towel or
blindfold for Zen or no items for a naked ascension).

